{"name":"Count’s Wardrobe","hex":"#772277","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"count","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To recite numbers in sequence.","To determine the number of (objects in a group)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of counting or tallying a quantity.","The result of a tally that reveals the number of items in a set; a quantity counted."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/count"},{"term":"wardrobe","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A room for keeping clothes and armor safe, particularly a dressing room or walk-in closet beside a bedroom.","A governmental office or department in a monarchy which purchases, keeps, and cares for royal clothes."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To act as a wardrobe department; to provide clothing or sets of clothes.","To order clothing online and return it for a refund after having worn it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wardrobe"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wardrobe","blocks":[{"text":"A wardrobe, also called armoire or almirah, is a piece of standing furniture used for storing clothes. The term is also sometimes used for a closet. The earliest wardrobe was likely a chest, but the term wardrobe was first given to a room in which garments were stored, then to freestanding furniture with the same function, sometimes called a clothes-press. From these closets and cupboards the modern wardrobe, with its hanging spaces, sliding shelves and drawers, evolved gradually."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wardrobe","wikidata":"Q106106"},{"title":"Count","blocks":[{"text":"Count or Countess is a historical title of nobility in certain European countries, varying in relative status, generally of middling rank in the hierarchy of nobility. Especially in earlier medieval periods the term often implied not only a certain status, but also that the count had specific responsibilities or offices. The etymologically related English term \"county\" denoted the territories associated with some countships, but not all."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Johan_Erik_Lindh_-_Portrait_of_Carl_Gustaf_Mannerheim_%281797-1854%29.jpg/330px-Johan_Erik_Lindh_-_Portrait_of_Carl_Gustaf_Mannerheim_%281797-1854%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Count","wikidata":"Q3519259"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Kas%2C_early_19th_century.jpg/500px-Kas%2C_early_19th_century.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Kas%2C_early_19th_century.jpg/1280px-Kas%2C_early_19th_century.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Kas%2C_early_19th_century.jpg/500px-Kas%2C_early_19th_century.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kas, early 19th century, Brooklyn Museum (New York City)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKas%2C_early_19th_century.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Mus%C3%A9e_pyreneen.jpg/250px-Mus%C3%A9e_pyreneen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Mus%C3%A9e_pyreneen.jpg/500px-Mus%C3%A9e_pyreneen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Mus%C3%A9e_pyreneen.jpg/250px-Mus%C3%A9e_pyreneen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMus%C3%A9e_pyreneen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Frankf._Nasenschrank.jpg/250px-Frankf._Nasenschrank.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_cam